# IMPORTANT: the matmul kernels (kernels/r8_cube_fused_xs.cpp +
# kernels/r8_cube2_matmul.cpp, KERNEL_FILES_CUBE) are built into a SEPARATE
# ascendc_library() target from the ordinary Vector kernels
# (KERNEL_FILES_VEC) — when both kinds of file are mixed in one
# ascendc_library() (the way it used to be), the host_bisheng_obj build falls
# over on MatmulChipCap::GetFeatures() ("non-type template argument is not a
# constant expression"), even though separately, as in the official
# 11_matmul_kernellaunch example (there it is always one file per library), it
# builds clean. Splitting the targets is a working workaround, found
# experimentally by comparing against that official example.
#
# ATTEMPT #2 (it was rolled back IN FULL — and that was a MISTAKE, see below):
# adding HAVE_WORKSPACE/HAVE_TILING GLOBALLY (to both libraries at once)
# broke the build of the vec kernels — in auto_gen_*.cpp those macros
# intercept the LAST two parameters of a kernel and treat them as the GM
# addresses of workspace/tiling (the convention of the official
# matmul_custom(a, b, c, workspace, tilingGm)). The vec kernels have scalars
# as their last parameters (uint32_t full_size/num_stages/wTotal), hence an
# incompatible integer-to-pointer conversion. The defines were then taken out
# ALTOGETHER — including from the cube library, which cannot live without
# them, and that cost many hours of debugging:
#
#   WITHOUT -DHAVE_WORKSPACE the autogenerated wrapper does NOT call
#   matmul::clearWorkspace(), and the message-exchange area between the AIC
#   and AIV halves of the MIX kernel (KFC: MatmulClient on AIV <->
#   MatmulServer on AIC) stays uninitialized garbage out of the system
#   workspace. The halves of the matmul never come to an agreement => a REAL
#   hardware deadlock on the very first launch of fft8_cube1_matmul (in the
#   device log, SyncTask/LogicCqReportV2 "timeout" round and round, the
#   process at ~0 CPU%).
#
# The RIGHT solution is the defines SCOPED, to the cube library only (in the
# cube library's files both kernels deliberately have GM_ADDR workspace,
# GM_ADDR tilingGm as their last parameters). The vec library is built as
# before, without those defines, and nothing breaks.
#
# CUBE_BATCH is a story of its own. That define, unlike
# HAVE_WORKSPACE/HAVE_TILING, is harmless to any library: it is simply a
# number (the batch width of the matmul), and BOTH need it — the vec kernels
# r8_cube1_repack/r8_cube1_unpack lay out exactly the same batches the cube
# kernel computes. So we set it globally on both pipelines, while
# workspace/tiling stay scoped to cube alone as before.
# The mechanism for giving ascendc_library() its defines is one and the same
# in both cases (the helper, or else the DEFINITIONS property of the
# interface target), so it is wrapped in a shared function.
function(oddfft_ascendc_defs lib)
    if(COMMAND ascendc_compile_definitions)
        ascendc_compile_definitions(${lib} PRIVATE ${ARGN})
    else()
        set_property(TARGET ${lib}_interface APPEND PROPERTY DEFINITIONS ${ARGN})
    endif()
endfunction()

ascendc_library(ascendc_kernels_${RUN_MODE}_vec SHARED ${KERNEL_FILES_VEC})


# The cube library of the power-of-two project is deliberately absent here:
# phase 1 of the odd-radix project has no matmul kernels, so there is nothing
# that needs -DHAVE_WORKSPACE/-DHAVE_TILING or its own ascendc_library(). When
# the `cube` butterfly variants arrive, lift that block back from
# FFTBench/common/cmake/npu_lib.cmake unchanged — including the scoping of
# those two defines to the cube target only, which is load-bearing.
